# Eliminating A₈ on the permutation module: terminal displacement

Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*, combined manuscript
(1 March 2004), §12.6, printed pp.822–838 (PDF pp.839–855).
The section extends through p.838, including the end of Lemma 12.6.33 and
Theorem 12.6.34: under Hypothesis 12.2.3, if the action quotient
`L̄ = L/C_L(V)` is isomorphic to `L₄(2)`, then `V` is its natural module.
The source establishes `C_L(V) = O₂(L)` in this case. Sections 12.8 and 12.9
use this conclusion
to eliminate alternative 12.2.2(3d).

This module currently proves the final displacement contradiction from the
actual quotient action, the two invariant factors and the geometric
commutator containment. The original section assumptions now also give
`C_L(V) = O₂(L) = O₂,Z(L)`, with a compatible marking on `L/O₂(L)`.
The nonsingular A₆ quotient of L_v is constructed through the original
projection, and L_v≤K_v follows from its perfect and prime-to-three
residuals. The augmentation cover also identifies the full point-centralizer
preimage, giving index 28 in L. From the earlier 12.2.2 invariance and TI
clauses, an actual L-conjugate now has its T-centralizer Sylow in M_v and
index four in T. The local residual factorization, both 12.6.4 index bounds,
the chosen normalizer intersection, and all three 12.6.5 clauses follow.
The actual moved subgroup V_v=[V,L_v] now has an equivariant five-dimensional
core marking, with order 32 and trivial intersection with C_V(L). Its local
fixed line has order two and projects to v under the original quotient map.
This gives m₂(K_v)≥5 and the fixed-space consequence of moving that line.
The local conjugation kernel is O₂(L_v), and V_v is reduced in L_v.
With the earlier T-invariance premise, it is also reduced in C_LT(v),
whose centralizer on V_v is precisely R_v. In particular
V_v≤Ω₁(Z(R_v)); both O₂(L_v) and R₂(L_v) are moved nontrivially by L_v.
The actual weight-two vectors in this core marking now give L(v)=L:
their point residuals generate the original L, including its two-core.
The preferred escaping point is now chosen through the original augmentation
cover and placed in the source Sylow position. Under the contradiction
assumption L_v=K_v, C_{G_v}(V_v) normalizes each generating point residual,
so C_{G_v}(V_v)≤M. This discharges the permutation input in the first
reduction of 12.6.11. The same branch gives V_v≤O₂(G_v) and
C_{G_v}(O₂(G_v))≤M. Characteristic two of N_G(L) and M_v now gives
G_v∈He. The six-letter orbit calculation strengthens the centralizer bound
to N_{G_v}(V_v)≤M, so V_v is not normal in G_v. These are the two initial
reductions of 12.6.11. Both clauses of 12.6.7 now hold on the original
Thompson subgroups. Nontrivial chosen V and characteristicity supply the
normalizer bound N_G(J(Q))≤M from the earlier source uniqueness theorem,
so all three clauses of 12.6.8 have no additional normalizer premise.
The preferred escaping point inherits these conclusions. In the small
Thompson branch under L_v=K_v, its full G_v-conjugate span of V_v is a
strictly larger normal elementary subgroup inside Ω₁(Z(O₂(G_v))), moved
by J(T_v). Sylow-overgroup inheritance now proves that span is reduced.
The literal quotient-action centralizer of L_v has order prime to three
by the three-rank bound. Under L_v=K_v it is normal in G_v and contains
J(T_v). The complete B.5 classification and the maximum elementary
counting argument therefore make J(T_v) centralize the span, contradicting
the earlier movement assertion. This proves L_v<K_v at the preferred
point in the small Thompson branch. Local Sylow transport now proves
12.6.6 and 12.6.9: if J(T_v) centralizes V_v, it centralizes every V_u
for a weight-two point u in V_v, and hence V itself by 12.6.10. Thus it
lies in Q; otherwise L_v=[L_v,J(T_v)]. The preferred equal-residual
branch therefore has both local and global Thompson generation with
no generation premise. At that same preferred point, R₂(G_v) now lies
in T_v and R_v. A.4.11 proves that L_v moves it, and Thompson generation
supplies an FF-module on its literal centralizer quotient. The L_v-image
is identified with A₆ inside the full best-offender subgroup, so the proved
B.5 classifier applies to the original reduced action. The literal L_v
kernel on that module is O₂(L_v), so the centralizer of its A₆ image has
order prime to three. This excludes the product and outer A₆–C₃ rows of
B.5.6; the actor order excludes the reducible B.5.1 rows, making the actual
moved space Irr⁺. The two-group R_v centralizes this space by A.1.41.
Consequently V_v and [R₂(G_v),L_v] lie in the same normal elementary local
module, their literal join, whose centralizer inside C_LT(v) is exactly R_v.
The common local action now has an FF witness and the original A₆ component
image. B.5 makes its moved space Irr⁺, and the two actual fully moved
subgroups give [R₂(G_v),L_v]=V_v. In the equal-residual branch this makes
V_v normal in G_v, contradicting the earlier reduction. Thus the preferred
escaping point satisfies L_v<K_v. The original A₈ component has index prime
to three in M, so K_v≤M would put the prime-to-three residual K_v in L and
then, by two-perfectness, in L_v. This proves K_v not contained in M and
completes both clauses of 12.6.11 at that point. Its literal terminal consumer
now takes strict enlargement and constructs the escaping-component premise.
The original rank-five moved subgroup excludes the quasisimple M₂₃ row
of 12.6.12 using its actual multiplier and two-rank bounds. Self-duality
of the original natural section computes fixed codimension five for L_v;
the quotient projection and L_v≤K_v give corank at least five for C_V(K_v).
Component projections in the actual radical quotient now select the unique
normal C-component containing L_v. The prime-to-three residual of the layer
is this component, so the source layer-generation theorem identifies it with
the literal K_v=O^{3′}(G_v^∞). This proves every clause of 12.6.3 without
assuming A.3.18. The original A₆ section also excludes a cubic center in the
quasisimple two-core quotient, proving the simplicity clause of 12.6.13(2).
The preferred escaping-point consumer receives this same recognized K_v.
The central quotient now has two-rank at least four and Aut(K_v) has
two-rank at least five. These exclude the small alternating and
odd-characteristic rows, M₁₁, M₁₂ and J₁. The literal images of L_v and
V_v in K_v/Z(K_v) retain the A₆ quotient and a nontrivial normalized
elementary two-subgroup. The rank-one perfect-image theorems and rank-two
local covers exclude all Bender and rank-two models. The actual J₂
automorphism rank bound excludes J₂. Theorem C therefore leaves just
PSL₅(2), M₂₂, M₂₄, HS, He, Ru and J₄ at the same preferred escaping point.
The original conjugation image of V in Aut(K_v/Z(K_v)) has order at least
32 and is normalized by the perfect L_v-image, whose order is divisible by
nine. The native outer Sylow catalogue and quintet geometry rule this out
in Aut(M₂₂), excluding M₂₂ with the literal central projection. The same
preferred-point consumer initially retains PSL₅(2), M₂₄, HS, He, Ru and J₄.
The trivial PSL₅(2) multiplier preserves the order-32 moved subgroup, whose
A₆ normalizer contradicts the binary five-dimensional two-local bound.
The original local fixed line now excludes M₂₄ and Held through the literal
central projection: their odd covering kernels preserve its involution.
For M₂₄ its centralizer has order prime to nine. In Held, the genuine
index-two PSL₃(4) central cover and parabolic geometry exclude the perfect
three-rank-two local image acting on a nontrivial moved two-subgroup.
The general preferred-point catalogue therefore retains only HS, Ru and J₄.
The source small-Thompson Sylow arrangement excludes HS and Ru, leaving
only J₄ in that subcase. In the Ru row the original fixed line is exactly
the kernel on V_v of the central projection, whose image has order sixteen.
In the HS row the intrinsic rank bound likewise gives moved-image order
sixteen. The multiplier bounds identify the original fixed line with the
entire center in both HS and Ru, of order two. The actual residual is
identified with the concrete perfect double cover by an isomorphism
commuting with its original central projection. At the same preferred point,
the simultaneous normalizer of the literal L_v and V_v images has index at
most four in a Sylow of the central quotient. This bound also holds under
any surjective model map out of K_v. These three remaining quasisimple
exclusions in 12.6.12 are still open.
In the escaping HS branch, the full original V-action has order 32 and
inner part exactly the order-16 moved image. The rank bounds identify
C_V(K_v) with C_V(L_v), so source normalizer uniqueness forces C_V(L)=1.
The original module then has order 64, V meets K_v in V_v, and both
C_V(K_v) and the ambient center of K_v are generated by v. In particular,
normalizer uniqueness also gives C_V(K_v)=<v> for every escaping K_v,
without assuming quasisimplicity. In the quasisimple case the original
local fixed line equals <v>. A nonidentity original L-fixed vector would
supply an actual inner involution in Ru or J4 centralizing both literal
local images, with simultaneous Sylow index at most two. The native Ru
centralizer orders and J4 extraspecial-core bound rule this out. Thus
nontrivial C_V(L) excludes all quasisimple rows at the preferred point.
The remaining HS, Ru and J4 rows have C_V(L)=1, |V|=64 and original
action image of order 32. In Ru all automorphisms are inner. The literal
L_v image normalizes the order-32 image of the original V, and its A6
quotient gives a nine-factor in its order. Three-group orbit counting
supplies a nonidentity involution fixed by its Sylow three-subgroup,
contradicting the native Ru involution-centralizer orders. This excludes
Ru at the original preferred point. Only HS and J4 remain, with the
compatible HS double-cover map and all original point geometry retained.
In the surviving HS configuration, L_v and the same S intersect K_v generate
all of K_v, retaining the full covering center. The same S preserves the
intersection and commutator of each original conjugate pair, while L_v
moves each pair's intersection, commutator and join. This restricts the
surviving configuration; its exclusion still requires an ambient argument.
For nontrivial original C_V(L), Thompson generation now gives
[R₂(LT),L]=V. The original core's Sylow fixed vectors and the omega-span
splitting prove L centralizes Ω₁Z(T), as in 12.6.1(6). The exact residual
O^{3′}(M)=L and the ambient Sylow conjugator then carry K_v onto L and
K_v S onto LT in the contrary normalizer branch. Two-residual uniqueness
lifts A6 conjugacy through the normalizing two-core and aligns the original
L_v. The actual A6 centralizer bound in S8 then identifies R_v with the
whole relative quotient centralizer and aligns R_v as well. This proves
N_G(R_v) is not contained in M under actual nonquasisimplicity, and gives
Q<R_v at the preferred point when original C_V(L) is nontrivial.
The same normalizer theorem also supplies N_G(Q)≤M to the existing
terminal contradiction, whose block and splitting premises remain open.
Conjugation retains the original centralizer-control target; the 12.6.28
singular-subspace application remains conditional on control in this
selected position.
The local A₅-versus-A₈ actor contradiction for 12.6.23 retains the actual
conjugation actions and quotient maps. A nonzero fixed space now gives the
actual core marking over the original quotient; together with fusion, TI
and centralizer control, it supplies the intersection bound and r(G,V)≥4.

At the unchanged preferred point with nontrivial original C_V(L), the
actual A8-block now gives the A6-block L_v with U(L_v)=V_v. Its original
six-letter action and fixed quotient are identified with the natural A6
module. Short-seed propagation constructs the relative K_v-block in G_v,
and characteristic two gives V_v <= [U,K_v] without an R=R_v premise.
The preferred ambient-Sylow exclusion sharpens |S:T_v| to at most two.
The actual index-two Sylow bound now excludes both SL4(2) and SL5(2)
through the literal A6 section. The FF witness and full natural A7 moved module are constructed in this
branch, with S=T_v and R_v=O2(K_v T_v) split over that module and C_T_v(K_v).
The original Sylow centralizer is C_V(L), of order two. Commutator cocycles
on the literal L/O2(L) and V/C_V(L) now bound |Q| by256; the previously
constructed elementary subgroup of that order is therefore Q itself.
Two-perfectness of K_v forces it to normalize this index-two elementary
subgroup of the non-elementary R_v, contradicting normalizer uniqueness.
Thus nonsingular centralizers are contained in M when C_V(L) is nontrivial.
The general 12.6.13(3) containment is now proved under nonquasisimplicity,
without a fixed-space or R=R_v premise. In the fixed-free branch the actual
canonical FF module cannot have A7 component: its zero moved fixed space
contradicts the original local point line. Only SL4(2) and SL5(2) remain
under FF, and the same selected S must be ambient Sylow with |S:T_v|=4.
Both binary linear rows are now excluded under FF as well: the SL4(2)
whole orthogonal module has zero K_v-fixed intersection with the original
local moved five-space; every SL5(2) whole moved row has zero fixed space.
The fixed-free rank comparison now supplies FF on this same selected Sylow,
closing the escaping nonquasisimple branch. Only genuine quasisimple HS/J4
central-quotient models remain for full12.6.2.
For nontrivial fixed space, the centralizer premises of 12.6.20 are now
discharged: under the retained fusion premise, the original compatible core
marking puts every nonidentity intersection vector outside N_G(V) at weight
four, gives intersection rank at most three, and proves r(G,V)>=4.

No nonsingular vector now centralizes an ambient Sylow: centralizer control
excludes it through the orbit length28, while an escaping centralizer gives
an actual quasisimple component, contradicting even characteristic after
transport along the original nonsingular orbit. Thus, when C_V(L)=1,
the nonidentity singular vectors are precisely the two-central involutions.
This proves12.6.19(1) in that branch and the literal centralizer conjugator
of12.6.20(2), without assuming the remaining HS/J4 exclusions. The natural
six-space intersection bound and r(G,V)>=3 now retain only nonsingular
centralizer containment; their fusion premise is discharged.

When the original J(T) lies in Q, Burnside fusion through its controlled
normalizer now proves L controls G-fusion in V, also when C_V(L) is
nontrivial. The actual index |C_G(v):C_M(v)| is odd for every v in V.
The literal correcting conjugator and compatible core intersection theorem
therefore need no fusion premise in this branch. Any remaining fusion
failure forces C_V(L) nontrivial, J(T) outside Q, and L centralizing the
original central omega; its nonidentity point residuals are exactly L.

For nontrivial original fixed space, the compatible augmentation-core
marking now supplies the full preimage of the four-block radical. Its
literal action has kernel Q and image V4 x V4, so its index over Q is16.
The core fixed-space bound proves J(R)=J(Q) and N_G(R)<=M. An actual
LT-conjugator places this subgroup in the original T and retains all
these conclusions. The selected L-conjugate now has T4 Sylow in its
M-centralizer, index two in T, and O2(L1T4)=O2(QL1)=Rv. The normalizer
of its literal A4 x A4 image is the unordered four-block stabilizer.
Consequently an adjusted fusion conjugator fixing L1 and sending T4
into LT preserves the original core and lies in M; it cannot fuse the
weight-four point into C_G(L). The E9 quotient also places a residual
fused into a controlled centralizer inside L. The remaining L-conjugacy
of this fused residual is still required to complete the argument.

The remaining branch of the nonsingular-centralizer theorem 12.6.2, the
elimination of `C_V(L) ≠ 1`, the abelian-conjugate-span theorem 12.6.26,
weak closure, and the local alternatives through 12.6.33 remain prerequisites.
Their witnesses must be supplied before Theorem 12.6.34 can be asserted.
-/
